Programming a MikroElektronika board without a probe
====================================================

A patch against mikroe-uhb (https://github.com/thotypous/mikroe-uhb,
MIT), which is an open client for the USB HID bootloader MikroElektronika
ships in the flash of its development boards.  It is here for the same
reason tools/qemu-patches is: without it a board in this tree cannot be
reached at all.

os/inferno/stm32f407 is brought up on a clicker 2 for STM32, whose
manual offers three ways in.  Two of them -- mikroProg and ST-Link V2 --
go through CN3, of which it says "2x5 headers should be first soldered
on the CN3 connector pads", so on a stock board the programming header
is not fitted.  The third is the bootloader that is already there,
reached by pressing Reset and connecting within five seconds, and
MikroElektronika's own client for it is a Windows program.  mikroe-uhb
is the same protocol, reverse engineered from USB captures, and it
handles STM32F4XX.  It is written for Python 2; this makes it run on 3.

	curl -L https://github.com/thotypous/mikroe-uhb/archive/master.tar.gz \
		| tar xz
	cd mikroe-uhb-master
	patch -p1 < /path/to/tools/mikroe-uhb-patches/0001-run-on-Python-3.patch
	pip install hidapi			# not needed on Linux
	python3 mikroe-uhb -v /path/to/istm32f407.hex

and press Reset on the board within five seconds.  On Linux the client
opens the hidraw device itself and conf/51-mikroe-uhb.rules in the same
tree says how to let it; elsewhere it goes through hidapi.

To check the patch rather than trust it:

	python3 -m unittest -v mikroeuhb.tests.device

STM32Program there replays a capture of a real STM32 devkit being
programmed and asserts the client emits the same USB traffic byte for
byte.  tests.devkit and tests.bootinfo pass too, except RandomBootInfo,
which shells out to gcc -m32.

What the client knows about the flash layout is worth having even if it
is never run: STM32DevKit lays an F4 out as 4 blocks of 16K, one of 64K
and 6 of 128K and asserts the total equals BootStart, so on a 1M part
the bootloader is the last 128K sector from 0x080e0000 and the
application starts at 0x08000000.  os/inferno/stm32f407/NOTE has the
rest, including what the client rewrites in the image and why that is
harmless.
